WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Did life's chemistry need a special force that no laboratory flask could replace?

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.140
Many early nineteenth-century chemists thought so.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.840
They called it a vital force.

4
00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:17.317
Berzelius drew that wall sharply.

5
00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:21.497
Rocks and salts belonged to inorganic chemistry.

6
00:00:22.937 --> 00:00:25.657
Sugar, fat, and urea belonged to living bodies.

7
00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:29.577
Only life, he held, could make the second group.

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:37.060
Then, in 1828, Friedrich Wöhler tried to make the inorganic salt ammonium cyanate.

9
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.720
Urea crystallized instead.

10
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.080
On its face, he had made a body molecule without a body.

11
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.120
The wall cracked.

12
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.130
But Wöhler had not proved that every trace of life was absent.

13
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.930
His cyanate could be traced to animal materials.

14
00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.350
A skeptic could say life still hid in the starting material.

15
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:01.953
Here is the guiding rule.

16
00:01:03.153 --> 00:01:07.593
To test a claim that chemistry needs life, remove life and prove it is gone.

17
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.453
A test counts only as far as its evidence reaches.

18
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:16.833
Kolbe pushed the evidence farther in 1845.

19
00:01:18.193 --> 00:01:23.033
He built acetic acid from carbon disulfide made from charcoal and sulfur.

20
00:01:24.493 --> 00:01:27.373
Chemists then counted those starting materials as lifeless.

21
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:30.153
The second segment lit up.

22
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:36.403
One refuge remained: perhaps a whole process still needed an intact cell.

23
00:01:38.003 --> 00:01:40.683
Pasteur showed fermentation appeared with living yeast.

24
00:01:41.963 --> 00:01:46.623
He argued that sugar-to-alcohol fermentation could not be separated from the living

25
00:01:46.623 --> 00:01:47.623
cell.

26
00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:54.147
In 1896, Eduard Buchner ground yeast with sand and pressed out a juice with almost

27
00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.607
no intact cells. He added sugar.

28
00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:01.087
Carbon dioxide bubbles rose anyway.

29
00:02:02.407 --> 00:02:03.747
Had living scraps survived?

30
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.200
Predict: which tests could tell living survivors from a dissolved chemical?

31
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.460
Warming, a cell-trapping filter, or an antiseptic that kills cells?

32
00:02:19.983 --> 00:02:25.243
Gentle warming stopped fermentation, but heat could stop either cells or a fragile

33
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:28.043
chemical. It could not decide.

34
00:02:29.563 --> 00:02:30.843
Filtered juice still fermented.

35
00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:35.163
So did juice treated with chloroform, an antiseptic.

36
00:02:36.543 --> 00:02:40.623
Together those controls showed no living cells or scraps were needed.

37
00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:45.207
Buchner had removed the cell and tested whether it survived.

38
00:02:46.487 --> 00:02:48.047
The final segment lit up.

39
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:54.567
Across seventy years, the vital force became unnecessary, one molecule and process

40
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:55.567
at a time.

41
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.790
Living cells obey the same chemistry as everything else.

42
00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:04.210
Their reactions can be isolated, measured, and explained.

43
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.470
Testing a claim that chemistry needs life requires proving life is gone.
